This is a known breaking change. In .NET 10, MAUI defaults to **edge-to-edge** rendering on Android (following Android 15 requirements), which makes `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` ineffective — the window no longer resizes for the keyboard.

## Solution: Handle keyboard insets

**1. Ensure `adjustResize` is still set** in `Platforms/Android/AndroidManifest.xml`:

```xml
<activity android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" ... />
```

**2. Use `Padding` with `SafeAreaRegions` on your page/layout** so MAUI automatically adjusts for system insets (including the keyboard):

```xml
<Grid IgnoreSafeArea="False">
    <!-- Chat messages -->
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />

    <!-- Input bar at bottom -->
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" VerticalOptions="End">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

**3. If `SafeAreaRegions` alone isn't enough**, handle the IME inset explicitly with a platform-specific approach in your `MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener(this));
}

private class InsetsListener(MainActivity activity) : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View view, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Bottom padding = max of keyboard or nav bar height
        int bottomPx = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);

        if (activity.Window?.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content) is ViewGroup content
            && content.ChildCount > 0)
        {
            content.GetChildAt(0)!.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottomPx);
        }

        return insets;
    }
}
```

## Key takeaway

With edge-to-edge in .NET 10, the OS no longer shrinks your window — instead it sends **insets** telling you where the keyboard is. You respond by adding padding/margin so your content shifts up above the keyboard.

On **iOS**, `adjustResize`-style behavior still works automatically via the `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`, so no changes are needed there.
